Here's a trick to get this to work... call the `Publish` method. Seems that `.RelPermalink` doesn't publish the resource.

```text
{{ with .Resources.Get "a.jpg" }}
  {{ with . | resources.Copy "b.jpg" }}
    <img src="{{ .RelPermalink }}" width="{{ .Width }}" height="{{ .Height }}" alt="">
    {{ .Publish }}
  {{ end }}
{{ end }}
```

## Description

With `renderer.lighting = new DynamicLighting()`, a node material no longer picks up its environment map. A polished metal `MeshStandardNodeMaterial` under a bright `scene.environment` renders almost black, and the same scene with the default lighting reflects the environment as expected. Changing the environment's intensity changes no pixel, and the generated shader has no environment sampling.

The cause is `DynamicLightsNode.setupLightsNode()` in `examples/jsm/tsl/lighting/DynamicLightsNode.js` (line 156 on `dev`). It builds its light node list from `this._lights` only. Since #33702 ("WebGPURenderer: Refactor material lighting setup", merged for r185), the material's own lighting nodes (the environment from `scene.environment` or `envNode`, the ambient occlusion node, the light map node, and the node from `context.getGI`) no longer travel inside a per-material lights node. They travel in `builder.context.materialLightings`, and `LightsNode.setupLightsNode()` (`src/nodes/lighting/LightsNode.js`, lines 228 to 232 on `dev`) merges them into the light list:

```js
const materialLightings = builder.context.materialLightings;
const builtinLights = this.getBuiltinLights();
const lights = sortLights( [ ...materialLightings, ...builtinLights ] );
```

#33702 changed one line in `DynamicLightsNode.js` (`this._lightNodes = lightNodes;` became `return lightNodes;`) but the override never reads `builder.context.materialLightings`, so under `DynamicLighting` every material lighting is dropped. r184 does not have this problem because the merge happened in `NodeMaterial.setupLights()` (the old code called `builder.renderer.lighting.createNode( [ ...lightsN.getLights(), ...materialLightsNode ] )`, which `DynamicLightsNode` did honor through `this._lights`).

I confirmed the environment case in a browser: the mean luminance of the sphere region is 175.1 under the default lighting and 10.0 under `DynamicLighting`. For `aoNode`, light maps, and `getGI` I only read the code: they are pushed into the same `materialLightings` array by `NodeMaterial.setupMaterialLightings()`, so they take the same path, but I did not render them.

`ClusteredLightsNode` does not override `setupLightsNode()`, so it inherits the base merge and should not be affected. I did not render it.

## Reproduction steps

1. Open the live example or the code below in a WebGPU capable browser.
2. The sphere starts under the default lighting and reflects the room environment.
3. Press "Switch lighting". The renderer now uses `DynamicLighting`, and the sphere turns almost black, with only the dim directional light's highlight left.
4. Press the button again to go back, and the reflection returns.

## Code

```html
