### Is this about generics?

Yes, this can be related to numeric generics. One of my module has this.
```go
type Int interface {
	~int | ~int8 | ~int16 | ~int32 | ~int64
}
type UInt interface {
	~uint | ~uint8 | ~uint16 | ~uint32 | ~uint64 | ~uintptr
}
type Integer interface {
	Int | UInt
}
type Float interface {
	~float32 | ~float64
}
type Complex interface {
	~complex64 | ~complex128
}
type Real interface {
	Integer | Float
}
type Number interface {
	Real | Complex
}
```

If this proposal is implemented, one would not write such a long function for direct real-complex conversion, which is especially needed for mathematical operation in generic functions between real and complex numbers: 
```go
func ConvertFromReal[X Number, Y Real](y Y) (x X) {
	defer func() { recover() }()
	v := reflect.ValueOf(&x).Elem()
	if v.CanComplex() {
		v.SetComplex(complex(float64(y), 0))
	} else if v.CanFloat() {
		v.SetFloat(float64(y))
	} else if v.CanInt() {
		v.SetInt(int64(y))
	} else if v.CanUint() {
		v.SetUint(uint64(y))
	}
	return v.Interface().(X)
}
```

